><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-16.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>The PHE FA survey can be used as a tool to develop evidence informed PHEs to a specific female athlete population, rather than continue to use a single standardized PHE for all athletes as well as to evaluate PHE interventions/tools and changing health needs. For example, female adult athletes might be more interested in fertility and pregnancy than female youth athletes. &#xD;&#xD;Furthermore, the data obtained from the PHE FA survey can be used to identify and prioritize educational strategies to improve female athlete health literacy.&#xD;Data from our pilot study indicated that, given a choice,</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="5" rect="609.448975,566.858948,793.448975,658.858948"
/></highlight
